{'exec_id': 34, 'findings_note': 'Disability-led source (Scottish charity SC045492) running a real annual access survey, so the genre is right. Two reasons nothing was admitted: the 2023 report URL returns HTML rather than the PDF (logged, 15580 B), and the survey content is predominantly information provision, toilets and staff attitude rather than circulation geometry. || CORRECTED 2026-09-02: The two reasons given here are both wrong, and neither was knowable from what had been retrieved. (1) The URL did not return HTML instead of a PDF; it returned a Page-not-found page. The live report is one hop from the homepage at /accesssurvey and was retrieved in full on the same day (f07d3924aaec6708.pdf, 3344268 B, 29 pp). (2) The claim that the survey is predominantly information provision, toilets and staff attitude rather than circulation geometry was written from that 404 and is false: the report most-cited barrier is could not get around the venue (lack of lifts, narrow corridors, too little space or poor layout) at 56 percent, ahead of entrance access at 41 percent (p.13). The 6000+ respondents figure recorded alongside it is also unsupported; the report states over 4400. Admitted as REF-00978 / ACG-09 under the re-retrieval search logged the same day. The batch one disability-led source was screened out on a characterisation no retrieved payload supported - CLAUDE.md 2(c), against a Co-1 source, which CRPD Art 4.3 and R1 make co-primary. || CORRECTED 2026-09-02: R15 applied to my own writing: the ranking as first recorded OVER-CLAIMED. Full option set of Q7 (p.13-14) is parking 64, could-not-get-around-the-venue 56, could-not-participate 45, could-not-get-into-the-venue 41, no-suitable-toilet 41, staff-attitudes 34, facilities-not-as-expected 32, environment-uncomfortable 28, no-accessible-transport 22, staff-unsure-of-equipment 15, other 8, no-alternative-formats 4. So circulation geometry is NOT the most-cited barrier in the survey: parking at 64 percent outranks it. The defensible claim is that it is the most-cited IN-BUILDING barrier and second overall, ahead of entrance access and of accessible toilets which tie at 41 percent. Corrected before this batch reached canonical.'}
{'exec_id': 43, 'findings_note': 'R10 re-retrieval: a 404 is not a terminal answer. exec 34 tried euansguide.com/files/final-23-euansguide-results-pdf.pdf and received a Page-not-found page (logged 72c0fcb12f42614c.html, 15580 B). Four further fetches were logged; the homepage carries a link to /accesssurvey, which carries the live report on the charity S3. Retrieved f07d3924aaec6708.pdf, 3344268 B, 29 pp, sha256 f07d3924aaec6708a1246c6e545fb8c9c7927f9c79f230cd3b225c2e79daa940. HARM FINDING (R7): the most-cited barrier in the survey is Q7 option 2, could not get around the venue (lack of lifts, narrow corridors, too little space or poor layout), at 56 percent - ahead of could not get into the venue at 41 percent (p.13). Among 4400+ disabled respondents, circulation geometry is the most-reported failure class, and it outranks the entrance-focused barrier that accessibility practice most often addresses. || CORRECTED 2026-09-02: R15 applied to my own writing: the ranking as first recorded OVER-CLAIMED. Full option set of Q7 (p.13-14) is parking 64, could-not-get-around-the-venue 56, could-not-participate 45, could-not-get-into-the-venue 41, no-suitable-toilet 41, staff-attitudes 34, facilities-not-as-expected 32, environment-uncomfortable 28, no-accessible-transport 22, staff-unsure-of-equipment 15, other 8, no-alternative-formats 4. So circulation geometry is NOT the most-cited barrier in the survey: parking at 64 percent outranks it. The defensible claim is that it is the most-cited IN-BUILDING barrier and second overall, ahead of entrance access and of accessible toilets which tie at 41 percent. Corrected before this batch reached canonical.'}

{'match_id': 'session_2026-09-02-resea-REF-00978-ALL', 'source_ref': 'REF-00978', 'target_population': 'ALL', 'study_population': 'UK-wide self-selecting online survey, over 4,400 respondents. Q1 (p.11), multi-select: a disabled person 88%, family member or friend 13%, unpaid or family carer 9%, carer or PA 2%, someone of retirement age 11%, teacher/social worker/healthcare professional 1%, someone else 1%.', 'sample_size': 4400, 'match_grade': 'PARTIAL', 'mismatch_note': "THE SURVEY ASKS NO IMPAIRMENT-TYPE QUESTION, so no population is separable from the sample and every percentage is whole-sample. That is why the target is ALL and the grade is PARTIAL rather than EXACT: the source can say that 56% of a large disabled sample commonly meet in-building circulation barriers, and cannot say which populations carry that 56%. Roughly 12% of responses come from proxies (family, carer, PA, professional) rather than disabled people themselves, and the instrument does not let proxy responses be excluded from the barrier percentages. Self-selection through a disabled-access charity's own channels biases toward people who already encounter and report access barriers; the direction inflates prevalence and does not affect the RANKING of barriers against each other, which is what this source is admitted for.", 'created_at': '2026-09-02 21:34', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-02-research-batch-05-circulation-icf', 'ref_id': 'REF-00978', 'gap_id': None}
{'match_id': 'session_2026-09-02-resea-REF-00978-MOB', 'source_ref': 'REF-00978', 'target_population': 'MOB', 'study_population': 'Mobility-device use is reported only indirectly, via Q29 main modes of transport (p.22), multi-select: wheelchair 25%, mobility scooter 24%, powerchair 15%, wheelchair with a power attachment 6%, walking 12%, car owned or leased 91%.', 'sample_size': 4400, 'match_grade': 'PARTIAL', 'mismatch_note': 'Q29 is a transport question, not an impairment question, and it is NOT cross-tabulated against the Q7 barriers question. So mobility-device users are demonstrably a large share of the sample, but the 56% could-not-get-around-the-venue figure cannot be attributed to them specifically - it is the whole-sample figure. Graded PARTIAL for that reason. Using this source to anchor a MOB-specific value would assert a cross-tabulation the report does not publish.', 'created_at': '2026-09-02 21:34', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-02-research-batch-05-circulation-icf', 'ref_id': 'REF-00978', 'gap_id': None}
{'candidate_id': 73, 'exec_id': None, 'found_under_slug': 'accessible-circulation-geometry', 'suggested_slug': None, 'disposition': 'ADMITTED', 'title': 'Euan s Guide Access Survey (Euan s Guide, Scottish charity SC045492, disability-led)', 'locator': None, 'locator_status': None, 'tier_guess': 2, 'harm_finding': 0, 'why_not_admitted': 'The 2023 report URL returns HTML rather than the PDF, so the document itself was never retrieved.', 'notes': 'Genuinely disability-led with a decade of annual survey data and 6000+ respondents in 2023. Content skews to information provision, toilets and staff attitude rather than circulation. RETRIEVE THE ACTUAL PDF BEFORE JUDGING IT; if its circulation content is thin, say so and close it rather than leaving it open indefinitely. || RESOLVED 2026-09-02 (R15, re-described from the source; admitted as REF-00978): Retrieved in full (f07d3924aaec6708.pdf, 29 pp, published March 2026). Both halves of the staged description above are wrong. The respondent count is over 4,400, not 6,000+, and the report is the 2025 survey, not 2023. The content claim is false: Q7, barriers commonly found when out and about (p.13-14), ranks lack of appropriate parking 64 percent, could not get around the venue for want of lifts, corridor width, space or layout 56 percent, could not participate in the same way 45 percent, could not get into the venue 41 percent, no suitable toilet 41 percent, staff attitudes 34 percent. Circulation geometry is the most-cited in-building barrier and second of twelve overall, ahead of both the entrance and toilet options the staged description named it as skewing towards. The staged text was written from a Page-not-found page and no retrieved payload ever supported it. Q1 (p.11): 88 percent of respondents are disabled people; the survey asks no impairment-type question, so no population is separable and every figure is whole-sample.', 'session': 'session_2026-09-02-research-batch-05-circulation-icf', 'created_at': '2026-09-02 